a march of defiance through the streets  of Charleston the shock of this week  shooting has left residents determined  to keep going but asking exactly how it  came to be that nine people were gunned  down inside a church a family friend of  Survivor Felicia Sanders who watched her  26 year old son tywanza die said the  young man tried to negotiate with the  gunman he asked the other person told  the other person that was not necessary  to be doing what he was doing that's  after some of the people had already  been severely wounded tried to talk him  down try to talk peace he was ignored  the mother his last view of her son ty  was crawling over to his auntie and an  effort  and that's her final memory meanwhile a  racist manifesto has emerged online  alongside pictures of the white suspect  charged in connection with the  Charleston murders some images appear to  show 21 year old Dylan roof posing with  the Confederate flag and wax figures  depicting slaves the FBI is  investigating the authenticity of the  images South Carolina is the only u.s.  state that still allows the Confederate  flag to fly outside its Capitol building  many residents view it as a source of  historic pride others say it's a symbol  of slavery and white supremacy past  efforts to take down the flag have  failed but a new movement for its  removal is growing in the wake of this  week's racially motivated killings we  know the heat we know the danger it says  stop it says you are not welcome here it  says fear for your life take down the  flag  we are all members today the historic  emanuel african methodist church where  the massacre took place will reopen its  doors on sunday many hope the worship  will mark the beginning of recovery and  healing for charleston
 

